{
  "sequences": {
	"wavefunctions": {
	  "name": "Wavefunctions: Everything Oscillates",
	  "truth": "Everything oscillates. Fourier shows every signal is a sum of sines. The wave is the fundamental pattern of non-rest\u00e2\u20ac\u201dperpetual movement between poles.",
	  "qfep_term": "dynamics",
	  "qfep_connection": "Wavefunctions are the mathematical form of edge-of-chaos dynamics. Oscillation is neither static order nor chaotic randomness\u00e2\u20ac\u201dit is periodic motion at the boundary. In QFEP terms, waves drive the temporal evolution \u00cf\u2020\u00ce\u201dE(S,t) through rhythmic alternation.",
	  "formula": "sin(t) = projection of rotation, f(x) = \u00ce\u00a3 a\u00e2\u201a\u2122sin(n\u00cf\u2030x + \u00cf\u2020\u00e2\u201a\u2122)",
	  "demonstration": "See rotation become sine on the unit circle. Hear Fourier synthesis build complex sounds from simple waves. Watch the double pendulum transition from periodic to chaotic.",
	  "description": "The sine wave is the fundamental pattern of non-rest\u00e2\u20ac\u201dperpetual oscillation between poles. From rotation projected to line, to sound as material wave, to chaos as pattern breaking. Waves are the mathematical form of desire: always moving toward opposite, never settling.",
	  "layer": "oscillation",
